  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "💈 Barber Shop Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a barber shop you could paint on the window tomorrow."
    },
    "intro": "Good barber shop names are short, easy to say and fit on a window, a pole sign and an Instagram handle: a classic surname and \"Barbers\", a street name, or a pun customers repeat. The **Barber Shop Name Generator** suggests names for a barbershop, gents' barbers or mobile barber: catchy and clever, funny, classic old-school, modern one-word brands, luxury grooming rooms, your town or your own name.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good barber shop names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Harding & Sons Barbers</strong>: the classic family shape: it promises the shop has been here a while.</li><li><strong>King Street Barbershop</strong>: a street name tells people exactly where to find you.</li><li><strong>Bristle & Blade Barber Co.</strong>: two tools of the trade, and alliteration that's easy to remember.</li><li><strong>Fade Street</strong>: a modern name built on the haircut everyone asks for.</li><li><strong>The Razor Room</strong>: short, smart and suited to hot-towel shaves.</li><li><strong>Belgravia Grooming Club</strong>: a luxury name with a grand London district in it.</li><li><strong>Tache & Carry</strong>: a pun on cash and carry that customers will repeat.</li><li><strong>Stubble Trouble</strong>: a rhyme that's friendly rather than silly.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How barber shop names work",
        "content": "<p>Barbers have one of the oldest shop signs there is. The red and white pole goes back to the barber-surgeons, who cut hair and also let blood and pulled teeth: in London the barbers and surgeons were one company from 1540 until the surgeons split away in 1745. The stripes are usually said to stand for blood and bandages.</p><ul><li><strong>Classic</strong> names use a surname, \"& Sons\" or a street, and words like Barbers, Barbershop or Gentlemen's Barbers: <em>Keating's Barber Shop</em>, <em>Station Road Barbers</em>.</li><li><strong>Modern</strong> names take one trade word and add Co.: <em>Taper Barbershop</em>, <em>Fade Barber Co.</em></li><li><strong>Luxury</strong> names say rooms, house or club rather than shop: <em>Regent Shaving Rooms</em>, <em>The Grooming House</em>.</li><li><strong>Funny</strong> names lean on hair and shaving puns: <em>Hold Still Cuts</em>, <em>Hairy Tales</em>. Well-worn ones like \"Groom Service\" are used by many shops, so they're harder to stand out with.</li></ul><p>Choose <strong>Your town</strong> or <strong>Your name</strong> for names built from them, like <em>Hampton Shave Shop</em>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>Press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or paste one into the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>, to see whether the domain and social handles are free and to jump to the company register and trade mark searches. Then:</p><ul><li><strong>Search the company register.</strong> In the UK that's Companies House; in the US, your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Search trade marks</strong> with the UK Intellectual Property Office or the USPTO, especially if you'll sell products or open more than one site.</li><li><strong>Look locally.</strong> Search Google Maps for barbers near you: a similar name a few streets away will confuse customers even if it's legally fine.</li><li><strong>Look at handles with \"barber\" and \"barbers\".</strong> Customers type both, so try to get both.</li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For more on choosing and checking a name, read our guide: <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making the name work on the street",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Fit the window.</strong> A name of two or three words reads from across the road; a long pun doesn't.</li><li><strong>Put \"Barbers\" in it</strong> if the main word doesn't say what you do, so people searching maps find you.</li><li><strong>Think about the pole sign and the cape.</strong> A short name or initials works on both.</li></ul><p>Cutting hair for everyone? Try the <a href=\"hair-salon-name-generator.html\">Hair Salon Name Generator</a>. For grooming products, the <a href=\"skincare-brand-name-generator.html\">Skincare Brand Name Generator</a> has names for a beard oil or balm range.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_beauty-names.html\">beauty and wellness business name generators</a>.</p>"
